Setting Up Jenkins CI using GitHub Webhook with Jenkins
(Using Ngrok for public URL)

Step 1: Start Ngrok for Jenkins
Open the terminal where ngrok is installed and run:
ngrok http 8080
(Assuming Jenkins is running on port 8080)
Ngrok will generate a Forwarding URL, such as:
https://corkier-darla-handsome.ngrok-free.dev
This URL will be used in GitHub Webhook.

Step 2: Verify Ngrok Output
Ngrok terminal will display:
Forwarding URL (HTTPS)
Connection status
Active tunnels
Use the HTTPS URL only.
Step 3: Create a New Jenkins Job
Go to Jenkins dashboard
Click New Item
Enter job name
Select Freestyle project → OK
Configure:
Source Code Management → Git
Git Repo URL:
https://github.com/username/repository.git
Branch:
*/main
(Make sure GitHub branch = Jenkins branch)

Step 4: Enable GitHub Webhook Trigger
Go to:
Build Triggers → Select:
GitHub hook trigger for GITScm polling
Click Apply → Save

Step 5: Configure Webhook in GitHub
Open your GitHub repository
Go to Settings → Webhooks
Click Add Webhook
Enter:
Payload URL
https://corkier-darla-handsome.ngrok-free.dev/github-webhook/
(The suffix /github-webhook/ is mandatory)
Content type:
application/json
Trigger:
✔ Just the push event
Click Add Webhook
GitHub will test and show:
✓ 200 OK

Step 6: Trigger the Pipeline
Open any file in your GitHub repo
Edit the file
Click Commit changes

Step 7: Verify Jenkins Build
Go to Jenkins → your project.
You will see:
Started by GitHub push
The build will run automatically without manual triggering.
